@anon.53fe85e3a453dfbf0ab1e8743506e67c.1051 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1116 x i8] }> <{ [1116 x i8] c"Provides the current location in the document.\0A\0AYou can think of `here` as a low-level building block that directly extracts\0Athe current location from the active [context]. Some other functions use it\0Ainternally: For instance, `{counter.get()}` is equivalent to\0A`{counter.at(here())}`.\0A\0AWithin show rules on [locatable]($location/#locatable) elements, `{here()}`\0Awill match the location of the shown element.\0A\0AIf you want to display the current page number, refer to the documentation\0Aof the [`counter`] type. While `here` can be used to determine the physical\0Apage number, typically you want the logical page number that may, for\0Ainstance, have been reset after a preface.\0A\0A# Examples\0ADetermining the current position in the document in combination with\0A[`locate`]:\0A```example\0A#context [\0A  I am located at\0A  #here().position()\0A]\0A```\0A\0ARunning a [query] for elements before the current position:\0A```example\0A= Introduction\0A= Background\0A\0AThere are\0A#context query(\0A  selector(heading).before(here())\0A).len()\0Aheadings before me.\0A\0A= Conclusion\0A```\0ARefer to the [`selector`] type for more details on before/after selectors." }>, align 1
